1. Kafka Basics

Kafka Components:

Broker: Stores messages and serves producers/consumers.

Producer: Sends data to Kafka topics.

Consumer: Reads data from Kafka topics.

Zookeeper (if not KRaft): Manages broker metadata and cluster coordination.

Topics & Partitions: Core unit of message organization.

Key Kafka Concepts:

Partitioning for parallelism and scaling.

Replication for high availability.

Consumer groups for load balancing.

Retention policies: time-based or size-based.

2. Running Kafka on Kubernetes

Challenges:

Stateful nature: brokers need persistent storage.

Network configuration: brokers need stable hostnames/IPs.

Scaling: adding/removing brokers without downtime.

Zookeeper management (if using legacy Kafka versions).

Solutions:

Use StatefulSets for brokers.

PersistentVolumeClaims (PVCs) for durable storage.

Headless services for stable DNS names.

Resource requests/limits for brokers.

3. Kafka Operators

Operators are custom controllers that simplify Kafka deployment and lifecycle management in K8s.

Popular Operators:

Strimzi Kafka Operator

Confluent Operator

Banzai Cloud Kafka Operator

What Operators Do:

Automate broker deployment and configuration.

Handle scaling up/down.

Manage topics, users, ACLs.

Roll out upgrades with minimal downtime.

Automate failover and recovery.

Custom Resource Definitions (CRDs):

Kafka: Defines Kafka cluster.

KafkaTopic: Creates topics automatically.

KafkaUser: Manages ACLs and credentials.

4. Deployment Patterns

Single Cluster vs Multi Cluster

Multi-cluster for geo-redundancy.

HA & Replication

Minimize broker downtime.

Replication factor ≥ 3 recommended.

Monitoring & Logging

Prometheus + Grafana for metrics.

ELK/Fluentd for logs.

Security

TLS encryption, SASL auth.

Secrets management for credentials (K8s Secrets or Vault).

5. Scaling & Maintenance

Horizontal Scaling: Add brokers.

Partition Rebalancing: Reassign partitions.

Rolling Upgrades: Avoid downtime.

Backups & Restore: For disaster recovery.

6. CI/CD Integration

Kafka Operator CRDs can be managed via GitOps (ArgoCD/Flux).

Automate topic/user creation and configuration via K8s manifests.

Use pipelines to test cluster upgrades or scaling.

